EXPECTED FAILURE -- and the failure is the lesson, twice over.

Semi-implicit (linearly-implicit) Euler is A-STABLE, so unlike the RK4
sibling it does not oscillate to infinity -- but it is FIRST-ORDER, and on
the strongly nonlinear H2/O2 ignition transient one 1e-6 s linearised step
is wrong enough to drive the packed state unphysical.  The engine REFUSES:
the integrator reports it gave up (a positivity/finiteness violation on a
fixed step) and the reactor turns that report into a named error instead of
exit 0.

Until 2026-08-22 this exact run reached T = -1610 K and EXITED 0: EulerSI
ignored the declared positivity contract, the reactor DISCARDED the
integrator's own ok flag, and the thermo dutifully announced Cp
"extrapolated to -1610 K, still returned".  A finite absurdity passed the
NaN guard.  This case witnesses the refusal that closed all three holes --
and, with ignition01_h2o2 (Rosenbrock23) and ignition01_h2o2_rk4, completes
the integrator lesson: L-stable second order converges, explicit fourth
order blows up loudly, and A-stable first order fails HONESTLY.
